# SysManage Agent Sudoers Configuration for Ubuntu/Debian
# This file grants the sysmanage-agent user necessary privileges for system management
# File location: /etc/sudoers.d/sysmanage-agent
# Permissions: 0440 (enforced by package installer)

# Allow non-interactive sudo.
# (``Defaults:sysmanage-agent !requiretty`` used to live here but
# sudo 1.9.x removed ``requiretty`` entirely; non-interactive sudo
# is the default behavior now and the directive is rejected as an
# unknown setting on Ubuntu 26.04+, openSUSE Tumbleweed, etc.)

# Package Management (APT/DPKG)
#
# Grant the bare apt / apt-get binaries rather than scoping to
# individual subcommands.  sudo matches command arguments
# POSITIONALLY: a rule like ``/usr/bin/apt-get update`` only matches
# when ``update`` is the FIRST argument.  But the
# repository_mirroring_engine's setup_install plan runs
# ``apt-get -o Dpkg::Options::=--force-confold update`` (and the same
# ``-o`` prefix on ``install``) -- the option sits before the
# subcommand, so the positional rule never matches and sudo denies
# with "user not allowed to run" / "I'm afraid I can't do that".
# Bare-binary grant matches what the engine's docstring already
# assumes ("authorizes specific binaries: apt-get, dnf, zypper, pkg")
# and the same philosophy used for sed/rm/install below -- the agent's
# own code decides the argv; sudoers is the "which binary" gate.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt-get *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/dpkg -i *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/add-apt-repository *

# Snap package updates (apply_snap_updates).  ``snap refresh <name>``
# requires root to swap the system-wide snap symlinks.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/snap refresh *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /snap/bin/snap refresh *

# Flatpak system-installed app updates (apply_flatpak_updates).  User-
# scope flatpaks don't need sudo, but system-scope ones do; the agent
# can't always tell which scope a given bundle_id is in until it tries,
# so we authorize sudo for both call patterns.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/flatpak update -y *

# Firmware updates via fwupd (apply_fwupd_updates).  Writes to EFI vars
# / firmware partitions, which require root.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/fwupdmgr update *

# Distribution release upgrade (apply_ubuntu_release_updates).  Rewrites
# /etc/apt/sources.list and runs apt-get dist-upgrade under the hood.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/do-release-upgrade *

# Repository mirroring tools (Pro+ repository_mirroring_engine).  The
# engine's sync plan invokes these directly via sudo to keep
# /var/mirror/<name>/ in sync with upstream and to refresh distro
# metadata after each sync.  Without these rules, sync runs fail at
# the apt-mirror / createrepo step with "user not allowed".
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt-mirror
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/createrepo_c, /usr/sbin/createrepo
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/reposync
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/trickle

# Memory containment for mirror syncs (repository_mirroring_engine).
# apt-mirror (especially the ``apt-mirror2`` Python rewrite that ships
# as ``apt-mirror`` on recent distros) parses the full upstream package
# index into RAM per worker and can OOM a small mirror host, taking the
# sysmanage-agent down with it.  The engine wraps the sync in a
# transient systemd scope (``systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=…``) so a
# runaway sync is contained to a cgroup OOM instead of a machine-wide
# one.  Bare-binary grant (same philosophy as the tools above); the
# engine decides the argv and skips the wrap at run time when this
# grant is absent, so older hosts simply run uncapped.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemd-run, /bin/systemd-run

# Helper utilities the mirroring/snapshot/import plans run via sudo.
# mkdir creates the per-mirror tree before apt-mirror lays it out;
# find prunes partial-fetch ``.tmp`` files after a sync; chmod/chown
# fix permissions when a snapshot is read-only-frozen or restored;
# rsync copies the live mirror tree into .snapshots/<id>/ (snapshot
# action) and back out again (restore action).
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mkdir, /bin/mkdir
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/find
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/chmod, /bin/chmod
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/chown, /bin/chown
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/rsync

# Air-gap collector ISO build (Pro+ airgap_collector_engine).  The
# build_iso_plan step bundles the staging tree into an ISO with
# ``sudo xorriso`` and checksums it with ``sudo sha256sum`` (the
# manifest write uses ``sudo install``, already granted below).  Bare-
# binary grants, same philosophy as the mirroring tools: the engine
# decides the argv; sudoers only gates which binary may run as root.
# Without these the build hits the password prompt and the run dies at
# BUILDING_ISO.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/xorriso
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/sha256sum
# Optical-disc burn (build_iso_plan with a burn_device set): write the
# ISO with ``sudo growisofs`` and eject the disc with ``sudo eject``.
# The CVE/compliance snapshot fetch uses ``sudo curl``.  Bare-binary
# grants (any args) -- without these the burn stage / snapshot fetch
# hits a sudo password prompt and the run fails.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/growisofs
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/eject
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/curl

# Air-gap repository ingestion (Pro+ airgap_repository_engine).  The
# repository ingestion orchestrator dispatches a loop-mount of the
# transferred ISO read-only, rsyncs the verified payload into the
# local mirror tree, then unmounts.  Bare-binary grants (same
# philosophy as the mirroring tools above): the orchestrator's own
# code decides the argv; sudoers gates which binary may run as root.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mount, /bin/mount
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/umount, /bin/umount

# Repository File Management.
#
# sudo 1.9.x rejects wildcard characters that appear inside command
# arguments (it accepts them only at the end of a single argument
# and not inside path components).  The earlier form of these rules
# tried to restrict ``sed -i`` and ``rm -f`` to the apt sources.list.d
# tree using ``/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*`` patterns -- but that wildcard
# now causes the line to be silently rejected by sudo, so every plan
# that touches a repo file fails with "user not allowed to run".  The
# rules below grant the bare binaries.  The agent's own code restricts
# what paths it operates on; the sudoers file is the "what binaries
# can we invoke" layer, not the "what specific argv" layer.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /bin/sed, /usr/bin/sed
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /bin/rm, /usr/bin/rm

# Generic file deployment via ``sudo install`` (used by repository
# mirror apply, agent config push, and any other plan that uses the
# ``generic_deployment.deploy_files`` step).  Wildcards-in-argv
# constraints have been removed for the same reason as above --
# trust the agent's own path validation rather than sudoers patterns
# that newer sudo no longer accepts.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/install

# Systemd Service Management
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start *, /bin/systemctl start *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop *, /bin/systemctl stop *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart *, /bin/systemctl restart *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl enable *, /bin/systemctl enable *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl disable *, /bin/systemctl disable *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl is-active *, /bin/systemctl is-active *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl daemon-reload, /bin/systemctl daemon-reload

# Firewall Management (UFW)
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw allow *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw --force enable
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw disable
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw reload
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ufw status *

# Firewall Management (firewalld - if installed)
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/firewall-cmd *

# Ubuntu Pro Management
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pro attach *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pro detach *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pro enable *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pro disable *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pro status *
# Livepatch detail (patch state, applied fixes) for the Ubuntu Pro card: an
# unprivileged caller cannot read the daemon (read-only status query).
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /snap/bin/canonical-livepatch status *

# System Power Management
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /sbin/shutdown -r *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /sbin/shutdown -h *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/shutdown -r *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/shutdown -h *

# Certificate Management
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/update-ca-certificates
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/update-ca-trust extract

# Content Lifecycle Management (Phase 16/19) -- content-view publish, promote
# and serving.  These three were MISSING, and their absence made the whole
# feature non-functional on a real agent: every publish/promote/materialize
# plan opens with ``sudo test -d`` and died on it, so content-view publish had
# never once succeeded outside a harness that strips sudo.  Found by running
# the flow end-to-end against a live agent on 2026-08-25.
#
#   test  -- existence guard at the head of every plan.  A pure predicate: it
#            cannot write, execute or modify anything.  ``find`` is already
#            granted and discloses strictly more.
#   ln    -- the environment promotion ITSELF (``ln -sfn v<N> <env>``), the
#            atomic flip that repoints an environment at a version.
#   nginx -- validating the generated serving config before reload.
#
# NOTE there is deliberately NO ``sh`` here.  ``sudo sh -c`` is arbitrary root
# execution and would defeat the bare-binary policy this file exists to
# enforce; the apt index generation was rewritten to run unprivileged and be
# placed with ``install`` instead.

sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/test, /bin/test
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ln, /bin/ln
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/nginx, /usr/bin/nginx

# Hardware health reads (Phase 23.1) -- READ-ONLY, exact argument forms.
# An unprivileged agent cannot read drive health: the NVMe log needs the
# admin ioctl, smartctl cannot open a disk, lvs cannot take LVM's lock, and
# btrfs device stats / ipmitool need root -- every drive read "not judged"
# (2026-10-16).  The agent runs these, and only these, through sudo -n.  Both
# /usr/sbin and /usr/bin forms: distributions differ (and Fedora merged them).
# smartctl: one exact form per disk node (sda-sdz, nvme0-nvme15).  sudoers
# cannot wildcard part of an argument (sudo-rs rejects /dev/*), and a
# whole-argument * would also allow -s / -t, which change drive settings.
Cmnd_Alias SYSMANAGE_SMART_READ = /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sda, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdb, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdc, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdd, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sde, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdf, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdg, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdh, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdi, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdj, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdk, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdl, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdm, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdn, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdo, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdp, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdq, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdr, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sds, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdt, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdu, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdv, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdw, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdx, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdy, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdz, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme0, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme1, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme2, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme3, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme4, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme5, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme6, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme7, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme8, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme9, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme10, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme11, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme12, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme13, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme14, \
    /usr/sbin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme15, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sda, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdb, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdc, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdd, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sde, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdf, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdg, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdh, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdi, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdj, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdk, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdl, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdm, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdn, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdo, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdp, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdq, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdr, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sds, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdt, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdu, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdv, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdw, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdx, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdy, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/sdz, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme0, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme1, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme2, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme3, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme4, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme5, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme6, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme7, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme8, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme9, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme10, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme11, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme12, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme13, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme14, \
    /usr/bin/smartctl -j -H -A /dev/nvme15
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: SYSMANAGE_SMART_READ
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/lvs --reportformat json -o lv_name\,vg_name\,segtype\,lv_health_status\,sync_percent\,raid_mismatch_count\,lv_attr
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/lvs --reportformat json -o lv_name\,vg_name\,segtype\,lv_health_status\,sync_percent\,raid_mismatch_count\,lv_attr
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/btrfs filesystem show *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/btrfs filesystem show *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/btrfs device stats *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/btrfs device stats *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ipmitool sdr elist
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ipmitool sel elist
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ipmitool sel time get

# Hardware identity (Phase 23.2): the three root-only SMBIOS files under
# /sys/class/dmi/id/ (mode 0400), read with cat -- exact paths, no wildcard,
# so nothing else is readable.  Without these every packaged agent showed
# "No machine identified" (2026-10-17).  Both cat paths: usrmerged and not.
Cmnd_Alias SYSMANAGE_DMI_READ = /usr/bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/product_uuid, \
    /usr/bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/product_serial, \
    /usr/bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial, \
    /bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/product_uuid, \
    /bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/product_serial, \
    /bin/cat /sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: SYSMANAGE_DMI_READ

# Compliance evidence (server ROADMAP 24.4): the only root-only reads the
# compliance checks need -- the EFFECTIVE sshd settings (sshd -T loads the
# host keys), the loaded audit rules, and two root-only config files.  Exact
# argument forms, no wildcard; both usrmerged and split paths.
Cmnd_Alias SYSMANAGE_COMPLIANCE_READ = /usr/sbin/sshd -T, /sbin/sshd -T, \
    /usr/sbin/auditctl -l, /sbin/auditctl -l, \
    /usr/bin/cat /etc/audit/auditd.conf, /bin/cat /etc/audit/auditd.conf, \
    /usr/bin/cat /etc/aide.conf, /bin/cat /etc/aide.conf
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: SYSMANAGE_COMPLIANCE_READ

# Backups with restate (server ROADMAP Phase 24).  The agent starts the fixed
# restate-job@<policy>.<action> unit restate ships; the job file it runs was
# validated by the agent and installed root-owned with ``install`` above.
# ``--no-block`` comes before ``start``, so the ``start *`` rule does not
# cover it (and sudo refuses a partial wildcard like ``restate-job@*``; this
# grants nothing ``start *`` does not).  The seal rule lets a NAS's agent
# snapshot its backup dataset.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl --no-block start *, /bin/systemctl --no-block start *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/zfs snapshot *
# The btrfs seal takes a read-only snapshot of the repository subvolume
# (``btrfs subvolume snapshot -r <subvolume> <dir>/sysmanage-<run id>``); the
# agent validates both paths before it asks.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/btrfs subvolume snapshot -r *, /usr/sbin/btrfs subvolume snapshot -r *, /sbin/btrfs subvolume snapshot -r *
# "Prepare storage for this host" (started by an administrator from the
# Backups page): the repository becomes its own ZFS dataset or btrfs
# subvolume, exported to that one host only through an /etc/exports.d file
# (installed with ``install``, then ``exportfs -ra``).  The agent builds every
# argument from the policy it validated; ownership uses chown/chmod above.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/zfs create *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/btrfs subvolume create *, /usr/sbin/btrfs subvolume create *, /sbin/btrfs subvolume create *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/exportfs -ra
# Then the repository directory is made append-only for the hosts with the
# append-only flag (chattr +a), never the sticky bit: a Linux NFS client
# with fs.protected_regular=2 refuses files it made in a sticky directory.
# chattr -a first, as a flagged directory refuses chown/chmod.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/chattr +a *, /bin/chattr +a *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/chattr -a *, /bin/chattr -a *
# Seal retention: after each seal the storage host removes its own seals
# older than the target's "keep seals for" days, never the newest -- only
# ``<dataset>@sysmanage-<run id>`` snapshots it listed itself (ZFS, recursive:
# every host's child dataset was sealed too) or btrfs seals it recorded making.
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/zfs destroy -r *
sysmanage-agent ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/btrfs subvolume delete *, /usr/sbin/btrfs subvolume delete *, /sbin/btrfs subvolume delete *
